AeroTrace: Tamper‑Evident Traceability of Aviation Life‑Limited Parts on a Permissioned Blockchain
Abstract
Aircraft parts such as turbine blades have life limits in flight cycles and flight hours, yet their history is held as paper or PDF certificates by different organisations, and forged paperwork has reached real airline fleets. This report asks whether blockchain is a meaningful answer. It analyses the problem against earlier studies and a published decision model, then presents AeroTrace Simple: a working permissioned blockchain written in Python with a web dashboard. Five organisations sign, approve and seal every event in a part's life, and the safety rules are enforced in code. Fifteen automated tests and nine demonstrated fraud scenarios show that invalid actions are refused and that any edit to history is detected, even when every hash is recomputed.
1. Problem Statement
1.1 What is the real-world problem?
Some aircraft components, such as turbine blades, discs and landing-gear parts, are life-limited parts (LLPs). The manufacturer approves a limit in flight cycles (one take-off and one landing) and a limit in flight hours. When either limit is reached, the part must be retired permanently. Whether a used part is safe therefore depends on its complete history "back to birth": who made it, who has held it, which aircraft it flew on and how much it was used.
That history is spread across the manufacturer, airlines, maintenance organisations (MROs) and brokers. Each keeps its own records, and the history travels between them as paper or PDF release certificates. A convincing forgery looks exactly like a genuine certificate, and nobody sees the whole history. Real cases show the consequences. In 2023 the European regulator EASA warned that CFM56 engine parts supplied by the distributor AOG Technics came with falsified release certificates, and airlines had to check their fleets and stores [10]. In 1989 Partnair Flight 394 crashed off the coast of Denmark, killing 55 people; investigators found counterfeit bolts in the tail assembly [11].
Problem statement. Organisations that do not fully trust each other must share one reliable, tamper-evident history for every life-limited part, and enforce its safety limits, without depending on one company's database or on paper that can be forged.
1.2 Who are the stakeholders?
Stakeholder	Role in the part's life	What they need
Manufacturer (OEM)	Makes the part, creates its birth record and sets its life limits	Proof that a part is genuinely theirs; no cloned serial numbers
Airlines (operators)	Install parts and record flight cycles and hours	Confidence that a part is genuine and within its limits before fitting it
Maintenance organisations (MRO)	Inspect, repair and hold parts	A trustworthy history on which to base decisions
Brokers and distributors	Buy, store and resell used parts	A verifiable history that preserves the part's value
Regulator	Oversees safety and audits records	Read access to the full history and assurance that the rules are followed
Passengers and the public	Indirect	Aircraft that fly only with safe, genuine parts
1.3 How is it currently solved?
•	Each part travels with an authorised release certificate, such as an EASA Form 1 or an FAA Form 8130-3, issued on paper or as a PDF.
•	Each organisation keeps its own records in its own maintenance or inventory system. There is no shared view of a part's history.
•	Checking a used part means requesting documents, contacting previous owners and auditing paperwork by hand.
•	Weaknesses: records are fragmented, slow to verify and easy to forge or alter; an administrator can change a central database without anyone noticing; and nothing technical stops a retired part from being sold again with fresh paperwork.
