2.2 Does blockchain provide a meaningful solution?
The choice was tested step by step with the decision model of Wüst and Gervais [7]:
Question	Answer for this problem
Is shared state needed?	Yes: one history for each part
Are there several writers?	Yes: manufacturers, airlines, MROs and brokers all add events
Can one always-online trusted third party hold it?	Not acceptably: the parties are competitors, and one administrator could alter history silently
Are all writers known?	Yes: they are licensed aviation organisations
Are all writers trusted?	No: forgery and fraud are the problem itself
Is public verifiability needed?	No: verification by the members and the regulator is enough
Result	A permissioned (consortium) blockchain is justified
Blockchain is therefore meaningful here, but only in permissioned form. Open mining (Proof of Work) would add cost and make the data public for no benefit, because the members are known. It is equally important to state what blockchain cannot do: it secures the records, not the metal. A false entry signed by a member stays false, although it becomes accountable, and linking a physical part to its record needs physical marking such as Data Matrix codes or NFC tags.
3. Technical Implementation and Working Prototype
3.1 Architecture and tools
The prototype, AeroTrace Simple, is a working web application of about 1,300 lines in seven code files. The blockchain is written from scratch in Python; standard libraries provide SHA-256 (hashlib) and Ed25519 signatures (the cryptography library). A FastAPI server connects the network to a one-page dashboard written in HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Five fictional organisations form the consortium: AeroWorks OEM (manufacturer), SkyLink Airways and Northwind Regional Airlines (airlines), Precision MRO (maintenance) and the Demo Aviation Authority (regulator).
File	Responsibility
blockchain.py	SHA-256 fingerprints, Merkle root, Ed25519 signatures, transactions, blocks and the eight-check verification
rules.py	Members, permissions and the safety rules (the "smart contract"); rebuilding the state by replaying the chain
app.py	The network: the five steps of every action, the tamper test, the document check and the web API
static/	The dashboard: Overview, Parts, Actions, Documents and Ledger pages
test_simple.py	Fifteen automated tests
 
