3.2 The blockchain core
•	Fingerprints. Data is written as canonical JSON (keys sorted, no spaces) and hashed with SHA-256, so every peer computes the same 64-character fingerprint.
•	Merkle root. The fingerprints of a block's transactions are hashed in pairs up to one root, which is stored in the block header. Changing any transaction changes the root.
•	Signatures. Members sign the SHA-256 fingerprint of each transaction with Ed25519 ("hash-then-sign"). Private keys stay on the server and are never sent to the browser.
•	Blocks and the chain. A block holds its number, time, the previous block's hash and the Merkle root. Its own hash covers these fields, and the orderer signs it. Block 0 (the genesis block) stores the members, their public keys and the approval policy.
•	No database. The chain is saved as a JSON file. The current state of every part is rebuilt by replaying all blocks from block 0, so the chain is the single source of truth.
3.3 Consensus: how one action becomes a block
Every action passes through five steps (Network.submit in app.py):
•	Step 1 - Sign. The member signs the transaction.
•	Step 2 - Check the rules. If any rule fails, the action is rejected and nothing is written to the ledger.
•	Step 3 - Approve. Each member's peer re-runs the same rules and signs an approval. The policy stored in block 0 requires approval from at least three of the five peers, one of which must be the regulator.
•	Step 4 - Seal. The orderer builds the block, computes the Merkle root and block hash, links the block to the previous one and signs it.
•	Step 5 - Commit. The block is appended and the part's state is updated.
Because the members are known organisations, agreement comes from signed approvals rather than mining. This follows the endorse-order-commit model of Hyperledger Fabric [5], simplified for teaching.
3.4 Safety rules (the smart contract)
Rule	What it enforces
ROLE	Only the manufacturer registers parts; only airlines install, remove and record usage; the regulator is read-only
DUPLICATE_SERIAL	Each serial number can be registered only once (no cloned serials)
UNKNOWN_PART	A part without a birth record on the ledger cannot be used
NOT_HOLDER	Only the organisation that currently holds a part may act on it
WRONG_STATE	Actions follow the lifecycle: in stock, installed, removed
USAGE_ONLY_UP	Flight cycles and hours can only increase
Automatic retirement	Reaching either limit (cycles or hours, never added together) retires the part
RETIRED_FOREVER	A retired part can never be used again (the safety lock)
Certificates stay off the chain. Only a certificate's SHA-256 fingerprint is stored in the part's birth record, and the browser computes a file's fingerprint locally to compare it with the ledger.
4. Testing, Evaluation and Demonstration
4.1 Automated tests
Fifteen automated tests (pytest) cover every layer of the prototype. All 15 tests pass (last run on 19 November 2026).
Area	Tests	What is checked
Building blocks	3	One changed byte in the 4,146-byte sample certificate flips 141 of 256 bits; the Merkle root changes when any transaction changes; a signature fails with the wrong key or changed data
Network and rules	8	A fresh chain is valid and reloads from disk; only the manufacturer can register; duplicate serials, non-holders and negative usage are rejected and change nothing; reaching a limit retires the part, which then cannot be installed; a transfer followed by installation by the new holder works; every block carries five approvals
Tamper evidence	2	Editing history fails the Merkle, signature and approval checks; recomputing every hash still fails the signature checks; the replayed state equals the live state
Documents and web API	2	The original certificate matches, but the altered copy does not; the API rejects a wrong-role action and serves the dashboard
4.2 Fraud scenarios demonstrated
Scenario	Attempt	Observed result
Wrong role	The regulator tries to register a part	Rejected by ROLE
Cloned serial	The manufacturer registers an existing serial	Rejected by DUPLICATE_SERIAL
Not the holder	Northwind records usage on SkyLink's part	Rejected by NOT_HOLDER
Clock rollback	SkyLink enters -500 cycles	Rejected by USAGE_ONLY_UP
Reach the limit	SkyLink adds 800 cycles (4,200 to 5,000)	Accepted in a new block; the part is automatically RETIRED
Reuse a retired part	Northwind tries to install it	Rejected by RETIRED_FOREVER (safety lock)
Altered certificate	Check a copy with one character changed	MISMATCH: 141 of 256 bits differ
History rewritten	Change 4,200 cycles to 420 in an old block	Chain invalid: MERKLE_ROOT, TX_SIGNATURE and APPROVALS fail
Cover-up	Also recompute every hash after the edit	Still invalid: BLOCK_SIGNATURE, TX_SIGNATURE and APPROVALS fail
In every rejected case the number of blocks stayed the same: invalid actions never reach the ledger.
 
