4.3 Evaluation against the requirements
Requirement	How the prototype meets it
Trust	Every event is signed by a known member and approved by the peers, including the regulator
Transparency	All members see the same ledger and each part's passport, block by block
Traceability	The full back-to-birth history of a part is replayed from block 0
Tamper resistance	Hash links, Merkle roots and signatures make any edit detectable, even after the hashes are recomputed
Decentralised verification	Any member can re-run the eight checks over the whole chain
Multi-party coordination	Five organisations agree through a stored approval policy instead of one administrator
4.4 Demonstration
The prototype starts with a double-click on run.bat and opens at http://localhost:8100. A five-minute demonstration follows the menu: the Overview explains the five steps; Parts shows the passport of part ATX-CFM56-TB90210; Actions runs the five one-click fraud scenarios; Documents checks the original and the altered sample certificate; and Ledger verifies the whole chain and runs the tamper test.
4.5 Limitations and future work
•	The five peers run in one process on one computer. A real deployment would run a peer at each organisation, for example on Hyperledger Fabric.
•	The demo private keys are kept on the server. Real members would hold their own keys, ideally in hardware security modules.
•	The ledger cannot detect a counterfeit physical part. Data Matrix or NFC marking would link each part to its record.
•	The prototype is simplified: transfers take one step, there is no maintenance or quarantine workflow, and each block holds one transaction.
5. Conclusion
Life-limited aircraft parts need a history that several competing organisations can trust, and today that history rests on fragmented records and paper that can be forged. The analysis shows that a permissioned blockchain is a meaningful fit: there are many known but not fully trusted writers, no acceptable single owner of the data, and no need for public access. AeroTrace Simple demonstrates the idea end to end: signed events, approval by the members including the regulator, hash-linked blocks, safety rules in code and a document fingerprint check. The tests and demonstrations show that invalid actions are refused and any change to history is detected. The main open steps are real distributed peers, organisation-held keys and a physical link between each part and its record.
